<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Morgan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1635–<a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir Henry Morgan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Morgan was a Welsh privateer based at Port Royal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, who led raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> in 1668 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> in 1669. In 1671 he crossed the isthmus and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a>, which was rebuilt on a new site. He was later knighted and served as lieutenant governor of Jamaica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-morgan.jpg" alt="Henry Morgan" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre Exquemelin, Piratas de la America (1681) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morgan,Henry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Morgan was born about 1635 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>, in Llanrumney or Pencarn, in an area now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> or near it. Attempts to identify his parents have proved unsatisfactory, though several sources name his father as Robert Morgan, a farmer. It is not known how he reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. He was probably a member of the raiders led by Sir Christopher Myngs in the early 1660s, and in 1666 he married his cousin Mary Morgan at Port Royal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sir Thomas Modyford, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, depended on the revenue that about 1,500 privateers brought to the island. In 1667, as relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> worsened, he gave Morgan a letter of marque and the rank of admiral. His commission allowed attacks on Spanish ships at sea, not on land. He took Puerto del Príncipe, and in July 1668 captured the three castles and town of Porto Bello, losing 18 men. Exquemelin wrote that he used monks and nuns as a shield for scaling ladders, an account Morgan later won a libel suit against in England.</p><p>In January 1669 the flagship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> blew up during a council of war, killing over 200 crew, and the flotilla was too small for Cartagena. Morgan attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> instead. In December 1670 he took Old Providence and Chagres, and in January 1671 he led his men up the Chagres River towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a>, much of the way on foot through rainforest. The destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> came after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1670">Treaty of Madrid</a>, and Morgan was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in April 1672. He was received there as a hero, and was knighted in November 1674.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, Morgan served as lieutenant governor and on the Assembly until 1683 and acted as governor on three occasions. In 1678 and 1680 he declared martial law against a French threat and strengthened Port Royal. He owned three large plantations and led three campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> of Juan de Serras, without capturing him. At his death he held 131 Africans as slaves. He died on 25 August 1688 and was given a state funeral, with a 22-gun salute. The earthquake of 1692 sent his grave into the sea.</p><p>His reputation was shaped by Exquemelin's memoir, written, Cordingly argues, with a grudge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> plunder. Spanish reports of Porto Bello and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> do not mention torture, though torture at Panama is reliably reported. The Spanish regarded Morgan as a pirate, which he rejected, since he always held a commission from the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Morgan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lewis <b>Henry Morgan</b> read it as a central government; Richter argues that decisions are made locally, by community consensus.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Port Royal, base of buccaneers such as <b>Henry Morgan</b>, flourished until an earthquake destroyed it in 1692.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-jamaica">Colony of Jamaica</a></li><li><blockquote>The trade attracted attacks: <b>Henry Morgan</b> sacked Panama City in 1671, and it was rebuilt on a new site in 1673.</blockquote><a href="/state/real-audiencia-of-panama">Real Audiencia of Panama</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Llanrumney, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Port Maria La luz, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.52,-3.13&amp;year=1669" aria-label="Llanrumney on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4479 380 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry Morgan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4929 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4929 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.52,-3.13&amp;year=1669">What was here in 1669? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
